2 NOTE Henrik Mogensen Rosenvinge, d. 1583, mayor, Öresund customs. R. Belonged to one of Helsingør's patrician genera. Although not a large merchant such as Sander Leiel , he, together with his brother Jens, drove a significant trade; T
3 CONC hey thus had, at times, greater clothing deliveries to the court. – R., already 1544, had become a councilor, 1555 was appointed mayor. He then came in close contact with the Øresund interpreter Sander Leiel who had already been m
3 CONC ayor for a number of years. The brother Jens also came in close cooperation with Sander Leiel when he n.å. appointed to the Customs office. When this highly regarded and trusted man died 1560, the two brothers were his self-writte
3 CONC n heirs. However, it was provisionally settled in the brother Jens Mogensen Rosenvinges to become his successor in the Customs office, and R. was appointed at the beginning of 1564. In addition, both brothers were taken on board w
3 CONC hen the new sea dish was extracted 1560.
3 CONT While Sander Leiel and Jens Rosenvinge had been able to work in calm conditions on the reorganization of the Sundcustoms administration which had begun with the accession of Sander Leif to the Customs office, Mr R. was given the m
3 CONC uch more unpleasant and responsible task of Continue this work during the changing circumstances of seven years of war. However much his grocery morale has shrunk in this way, in the first year of the seven years of the war, as th
3 CONC e King's representative, he had to seize ships, demand forced loans, blackmail crews, etc. He was also given the most unpleasant task 1567 to present to the skippers of Peder Oxes ' invention: The loading duty, the biggest customs
3 CONC  burden so far had dared to charge them through the sunken fitting ships. On this occasion he seems to have acted with a great deal of flexibility and autonomy and by modifying the tariffs to have mitigated the great arbitrariness
3 CONC  with which the new customs duties may be imposed. After the seven Years ' War, he seems to have been very skilfull in disputing the increasingly demanding office. His relations in the highest circles exploited him to obtain vario
3 CONC us advantages for his city, whose mayor he remained until 1579 when the king granted his resignation because of his work as an Oresund customs – which eventually also included the financial Administration of the major construction
3 CONC  works at Kronborg – quite seized his time. As a customs officer, R., as his predecessor, was established with some farms in North Sealand, and already at an earlier stage he had some of Esrum's goods in a mortgage.
3 CONT Family
3 CONT Parents: Mayor of Helsingør Mogens Jensen R. (d. 1528) and Anna Pedersdatter. Married 1. Time around 1539 with Karine Villumsdatter Forbes, death at the latest 1572, d. of the citizen of Helsingør, later alderman in Helsingborg Vi
3 CONC llum F. (Death at the latest 1561). Married 2. Time 1572 with Birgitte Mortensdatter Schulte,-D. by Morten S. In Flensburg. – Father of Mogens Henriksen R. Brother of Jens Mogensen R.
3 CONT Ad
