2 CONT '''*I have found evidence of his marriage, and added his wife to the tree. See below. Karrie Anderson Welborn
2 CONT '''
2 CONT -----
2 CONT https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2352409X21001516
2 CONT
2 CONT "Peder Pedersen Winstrup (who is also known to have used the noble name, Himmelstierna). He was born c. 1647 and enrolled, 17 years old, at the Leiden University in 1664 as Petrus Wynstrupius Londa-Scanorum (Möller, 1991). Militar
2 CONC y matters were of more concern to Peder junior than theological ones, and while in Holland he studied fortification. He married, at latest in 1679, with Dorothea Sparre, acquiring the estate Södertou from his late father in-law th
2 CONC e same year. However, in 1680, the Great Reduction struck, whereby fiefs granted to the nobility were reclaimed to the Crown. The few written records that exist demonstrate the dire economic situation that Peder faced, resulting, 
2 CONC among others, in losing his father’s estate in Lundagård in central Lund. In a letter dated the 25th October 1697 at Kåseholm, Peder Winstrup, in which he applies for a position as professor of mathematics at Lund University, lame
2 CONC nts the destitution brought upon him by the Great Reduction and other events. He was not to be appointed (Weibull, 1877). The last written record concerning Peder Winstrup is dated to 10th of May 1701, when he and his wife attende
2 CONC d the baptism of Christian Gyllenpalm as godparents. At this time, he was living out of charity provided by his brother-in-law, Johan Gyllenpalm at Kåseholm (Weibull, 1877). It is not known when he died, but according to the Åkers
2 CONC tein’s genealogies, he was buried in the Cathedral, most probably in the family vault. With the death of Peder Winstrup, the male line of the noble family of Winstrup ended. With the results from the aDNA analysis at hand and the 
2 CONC genealogy, the only person able to provide a second-degree relative to Peder Winstrup through paternal lineage was his son, Peder. The foetus of a boy placed in the coffin could thus be the grandson of the bishop. It seems probabl
2 CONC e that the relatives would have had access to the crypt where the coffins of the Winstrup’s were stored and, thus, a possibility to deposit the foetus in one of the coffins, in this case that of Peder Winstrup."
2 CONT
