3 STAE Scotland
1 OCCU Thane of Kinaldi. Giftede sig til landet Pitsligo. Dræbt i slaget ved Abroath., Ridder & godsejer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ----
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sir William "1st of Kinaldie" Forbes''' and jure uxoris of Pitsligo was the son of Sir John "of the Black Lip" Forbes and his wife Elizabeth Kennedy
2 CONT
2 CONT William "1st of Kinaldie" Forbes married Agnes Fraser, daughter of William "2nd of Philorth" Fraser and Elenor Douglas[3] after the contract date of July 24, 1423 and they had at least the following children:
2 CONT
2 CONT #     Alexander "2nd of Pitsligo" Forbes His Heir who married Maria Hay, said to be a daughter of an Earl of Erroll.
2 CONT #     Malcolm "of Meikle Wardis" Forbes who disputed his brother Alexander "2nd of Pitsligo" Forbes right to succession to lands.
2 CONT #     Arthur Forbes who married about 1463, Beatrice Douglas, daughter of James Douglas and Beatrice Sinclair. Beatrice Douglas was the widow of William "1st Earl of Erroll" Hay.
2 CONT #     ______ Forbes married to James Menzies of Laskgoune, probably son of Gilbert Menzies, burgess of Aberdeen to whom Sir William sold lands.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William "1st of Kinaldie" Forbes died at the Battle of Arbroath in Jan 1445/46.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir William 's wife Agnes Fraser survived him. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT https://finnholbek.dk/showmedia.php?mediaID=1438&medialinkID=4213
2 CONT
2 CONT (s.30 = Adobe s.49) ”Four bastards of Pitsligo. Th sd. Sir Wm. Forbes of Pitsligo had four bastard sons; that is to say 
2 CONT #Wm. Came,
2 CONT # Wm. begg, 
2 CONT #Ewell Duncaon of Kemnay,  (s.31 = Adobe s.50) “Ervell Duncan of Romney, Pitsligo’s 3d bastard son, married and begat 
2 CONT ##Pat.   Patrick Forbes married Donaldsone, and begat 
2 CONT ### John Forbes of Carnay and 
2 CONT ### Wm. Forbes in Elesawill. … Wm. Forbess, in Elesawell married …”. 
2 CONT ## Fergus Forbeses. 
2 CONT # Lochland”
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir John Forbes ”with the black lip” (=”med den sorte læbe”). Født 13.. . Tilnavnet skal han have fået efter et mærke på hans ansigt. Opdraget af sin mor og p.g.a. sine bedrifter blev han efter sagnet ridder. Fik 3 uægte sønner fø
2 CONC r sit ægteskab, som blev stamfædre til linierne Auchintoull, Culquharie, Buchan og Argigh. I ægteskabet fik han 4 sønner, hvor den ældste af disse blev den ”1.Lord Forbes” – de øvrige blev stamfædre til linierne Pitsligo, Tolquhun
2 CONC  og Brux. I det 5.regeringsår (o.1395) for kong Robert (III) af Skotland, blev han konstitueret som hans majestæts ”Justiciary” (sheriff?) for området Aberdeen, og som retsmediciner for herredet. Han er 10/11 1404 vidne til en tra
2 CONC ktat udstedt af grevinde Isobel af Mar på området Bonjedworth til hendes nevø Thomas Douglas, hvor han benævnes som ”Joannes Forbes de eodem, miles”. Død før 20/11 1406. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://home20.inet.tele.dk/perhag/1000aner/ab292.htm (dead link)
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT One forum casts doubt about this individual, or at least his connection with a merchant named William Forbus, who later went to Denmark:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/1996-12/0851213061
2 CONT
2 CONT He is not easily identified in other sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT -----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From the English Wikipedia page on the Battle of Arbroath, in which, if he is the Forbes of Pitsligo so named, he died:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Arbroath
2 CONT
2 CONT The Battle of Arbroath was fought on January 24, 1445 (or by another version in 1446) at Arbroath in Scotland. It was between rivals claimants to the post of Baillie of the Regality.
2 CONT
2 CONT Members of Clan Oliphant fought on the side of the Clan Ogilvy who were also supported by men from the Clan Seton, Clan Gordon and men from the Clan Forbes of Pitsligo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Their enemy was the Master of Crawford and his Clan Lindsay who advanced with over one thousand men. His father, the Earl of Crawford, rode between the two armies in an attempt to call a truce. However, an ill-advised Ogilvie, thi
2 CONC nking that this was the start of the Lindsay's attack, threw his spear at the Earl, hitting him in the mouth and killing him instantly.
2 CONT
2 CONT So began the battle, which went in Clan Lindsay's favour. Here fell Ogilvie of Inverquharty, '''Forbes of Pitsligo''', Brucklay of Gartley, Gordon of Borrowfield, and Sir John Oliphant of Aberdalgie (father of Laurence, first Lord
2 CONC  Oliphant), along with 500 or so Ogilvis. However, the Lindsays lost a disproportionate amount of men, most notably the Earl himself.
2 CONT
2 CONT External links
2 CONT
2 CONT Article on the Abbey of Arbroath
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.undiscoveredscotland.co.uk/arbroath/arbroathabbey/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT If 1320 was the Abbey's high point, its low point came on 29 January 1446. By this time the Abbot usually delegated most of his non-religious functions, powers and privileges to a nominated "Bailie of the Regality". This was a hug
2 CONC ely lucrative and highly sought-after position that tended to reside with the Ogilvy family. At the beginning of 1446 the Abbot appointed James Ogilvy to the role. A rival, Alexander Lindsay, arrived in Arbroath with a large crowd
2 CONC  of supporters to challenge the appointment. The result was the "Battle of Arbroath", fought in front of the Abbey and through the streets of the town. Some 600 people were killed.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ===References
