0 @N6000000003043307010@ NOTE [Burke's Peerage 1891, paraphrased:]
1 CONT
1 CONT John of Forbes, who besides Forbes (which he held from the Crown) held Edinbanchory and Craiglogie from the Earl of Mar and was Sheriff of Aberdeen; married Margaret [and their son was:]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir John Forbes of that Ilk, who succeeded before 1387 ... further acquisitions ... was knighted about 1390, died 1406.
1 CONT
1 CONT [ed note: Anderson, below, seems to have merged the above father and son]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [Anderson, W. The Scottish Nation. 3v. 1862, slightly edited:]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir John's posthumous son, Sir Alexander de Forbes, acquired from Thomas, earl of Mar, several lands in Aberdeenshire, the grant of which King Robert the Second ratified by his charter in the third year of his reign. By King Rober
1 CONC t the Third he was appointed justiciary of Aberdeen, and coroner of that county. He is witness to a charter of Isobel, countess of Mar, of the lands of Bonjedworth to Thomas Douglas, her nephew, of date 10 November 1404. He died t
1 CONC he following year. By his wife, a daughter of Kennedy of Dunure, he had four sons, namely, Sir Alexander, his successor, the first Lord Forbes; Sir William, ancestor of the Lords Pitsligo; Sir John, who obtained the thanedom of Fo
1 CONC rmartine (which now gives this title of viscount to the earl of Aberdeen) and the lands of Tolquhoun, by his marriage with Marjory, daughter and heiress of Sir Henry Preston of Formartine, knight, (of the Dingwall family,) and was
1 CONC  ancestor of the Forbeses of Tolquhouns, Foveran, Watertoun, Culloden, and others of the name; and Alexander, founder of the family of Brux, and others.
0 @N6000000003839142140@ NOTE
1 CONT Sir Alexander de Forbes, when King James I. was a prisoner in England, led a following of a hundred horse and forty spearmen to France, where he fought against the English under Henry V. at the battle of Beauge in 1421 and is immo
1 CONC rtalised by the poet Ariosto. Later in life—some time between 1436 and 1442 —he was created a Lord of Parliament by James II.  The precise date of creation is not known, but in a precept, directed by James II to the lords of the e
1 CONC xchequer, dated 12th July 1442, he is styled Lord Forbes. He died in 1448. By his wife, Lady Elizabeth (sometimes called Lady Mary) Douglas, only daughter of George, Earl of Angus, and grand-daughter of King Robert II, he had two 
1 CONC sons and three daughters.
0 @N6000000003043307039@ NOTE Alexander, founder of the family of Brux, and others
1 CONT
1 CONT [Anderson, W. The Scottish Nation. 3v. 1862, slightly edited.]
